      "text": "To find the median and mean, we first need to understand the given set of data. The set is \\(\\{n, n + 5, n + 6, n + 9, n + 15\\}\\). The problem states that the median of this set is 9. The median is the middle value when the numbers are sorted in ascending order.\n\nLet's denote the sorted set as \\(\\{a, b, c, d, e\\}\\), where \\(a \\leq b \\leq c \\leq d \\leq e\\). Given that the median is 9, we know that:\n\n\\[ c = 9 \\]\n\nSo, we have:\n\n\\[ \\{n, n+5, n+6, n+9, n+15\\} = \\{n, n+5, n+6, 9, n+15\\} \\]\n\nFrom this, we can deduce that \\(9\\) is the median, and hence \\(n+6 = 9\\). Solving for \\(n\\):\n\n\\[ n + 6 = 9 \\]\n\\[ n = 9 - 6 \\]\n\\[ n = 3 \\]\n\nHaving determined \\(n\\), we can now find the mean. The mean of a set of numbers is the sum of the numbers divided by the count of the numbers. So, we need to compute the sum of the set \\(\\{3, 8, 9, 12, 18\\}\\) and then divide by 5:\n\n\\[ \\text{Sum} = 3 + 8 + 9 + 12 + 18 = 50 \\]\n\\[ \\text{Mean} = \\frac{50}{5} = 10 \\]\n\nPutting this into code for verification:\n\n```python\n# Define the value of n\nn = 3\n\n# Define the set of numbers\ndata = [n, n + 5, n + 6, n + 9, n + 15]\n\n# Sort the data in ascending order\ndata_sorted = sorted(data)\n\n# Calculate the mean\nmean = sum(data) / len(data)\n\nprint((data_sorted, mean))\n```\n```output\n([3, 8, 9, 12, 18], 10.0)\n```\nThe sorted set of numbers is \\([3, 8, 9, 12, 18]\\) and the mean is \\(\\boxed{10}\\).",
